<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Dart mutation config — tier 1b on the Flutter side. Run from app/:

    dart run mutation_test -f md -o tmp/mutation mutation_test.xml

  Measured 2026-08-26 on an M-series laptop: 39 mutants, 1m32s wall
  (~2.4s per mutant), 36 detected, 3 survivors, all three equivalent.
  Pass `-o` or the report lands in the working directory.

  ## The builtin rules stay on, and that is the whole reason this is an input
  ## document rather than a --rules file

  `--rules` *disables* the builtin rule set unless `-b` is also passed. A
  hand-written rule set here would therefore be a weaker tier than having no
  file at all — a config that mutates `+` into `-` and calls itself evidence.
  Passing this file as a plain input document keeps `mutation_test -g`'s rules
  and adds the four things below that the builtins cannot know: which files,
  which tests, what counts as a pass, and what is not worth mutating.

  ## What was actually wrong with running it bare

  Not that it was green by construction. Bare, the tool reads pubspec.yaml,
  correctly infers `flutter test` — the *whole* suite — attaches a 60s timeout
  it does not mention, and points the builtin rules at all of lib/. Measured:
  4,176 mutants over 216 files, and 37s for one full-suite run. That is roughly
  43 hours, sequential, and it is a gate nobody can afford to be blocked by.

  Worse, the 60s inferred timeout sits 23s above a 37s baseline. A timeout
  counts as an **undetected** mutation, not a detected one: `undetected` is
  `totalRuns - totalFound`, and a timeout increments only the first. So on a
  loaded machine the bare run does not fail loudly — it reports a worse score
  and never says why.

  ## Scope: the mutation set and the test command name the same directory

  Widening one without the other is how this config becomes a lie. Mutate a
  file no listed test exercises and every mutant survives for a reason that has
  nothing to do with the tests; run tests broader than what is mutated and each
  mutant costs more for identical signal. Adding a directory here is two edits,
  always together: its sources into <files>, its test path into <command>.

  ## If a run is interrupted

  The tool mutates the source file in place and rewrites the original when the
  file is done, so a killed run leaves a mutated .dart on disk. `git status` will
  show it; `git checkout app/lib` restores it.
-->
<mutations version="1.2">

  <files>
    <!-- Pure policy: a function of its arguments, no Canvas, no clock, no IO,
         and one test file each. This is where a survivor means a missing
         assertion rather than an untestable seam. -->
    <file>lib/features/round/policy/answer_draft.dart</file>
    <file>lib/features/round/policy/diagnose.dart</file>
    <file>lib/features/round/policy/grading.dart</file>
    <file>lib/features/round/policy/prompt_layout.dart</file>
    <file>lib/features/round/policy/series_plan.dart</file>
    <file>lib/features/round/policy/streak_policy.dart</file>
  </files>

  <commands>
    <!-- Only the tests covering the files above. The full suite per mutant
         would multiply a 92-second run by about twenty-four for no new signal.

         The timeout is explicit and generous because the default is neither:
         120s against a ~2s baseline leaves room for a mutant that makes a loop
         run long, without letting one run forever, and without a slow machine
         quietly converting a detected mutant into a survivor. -->
    <command group="test" expected-return="0" working-directory="."
      timeout="120">flutter test test/features/round/policy</command>
  </commands>

  <exclude>
    <!-- `assert` is a debug-only contract. Mutating one changes behaviour a
         release build does not have, and the suite runs under the same asserts,
         so a mutated assert measures the assert rather than the tests. -->
    <regex pattern="assert\(.*?\);" dotAll="true"/>
  </exclude>

  <!-- The gate, and it is a real one: `mutation_test` exits non-zero when the
       detected fraction falls below `failure`. Verified rather than assumed —
       the same run scored 92.31% and exited 255 against failure="100", and 0
       against the 92 below. The metrics tool next door needed
       `--set-exit-on-violation-level` to stop exiting 0 on its own findings;
       this tool needs no flag, which is worth knowing before someone adds one.

       92 is what this directory scores today, so the next mutant to survive is
       a decision somebody makes on purpose: 39 mutants means one new survivor
       drops the run to 89.74% and fails. It ratchets up as the tests get
       sharper and never down to accommodate them.

       That last sentence has teeth when the file list grows. 92 is derived from
       *these six files*; adding a directory changes the denominator, and a new
       directory scoring 85% makes lowering `failure` to 85 look like
       bookkeeping. It is not — it un-ratchets round/policy by seven points in
       one edit. Re-derive `failure` from the new combined run, and only upward:
       a directory that cannot clear the standing number is a directory whose
       tests are not ready to be in here yet. -->

  <threshold failure="92">
    <rating over="100" name="A"/>
    <rating over="92" name="B"/>
    <rating over="80" name="C"/>
    <rating over="60" name="D"/>
    <rating over="40" name="E"/>
    <rating over="0" name="F"/>
  </threshold>

</mutations>
